<body>

<div class="top-bar">
  <div class="agency">National Aeronautics and Space Administration</div>
  <div class="nasa-logo">NASA</div>
</div>

<div class="header-green">
  <div class="postdoc">Postdoc Research</div>
  <h1>Global Significance of Serpentine on Mars</h1>
  <div class="authors">
    Author: A. Emran (3223 JPL Postdoctoral Fellow)<br>
    J. D. Tarnas (Blue); and K. M. Stack (3223)
  </div>
</div>

<div class="content-wrap">

  <div class="section-title">INTRODUCTION</div>
  <div class="two-col">
    <div style="flex:1.3;">
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li>Serpentine is an astrobiologically compelling mineral on Mars. H<sub>2</sub> and CH<sub>4</sub> (in presence of CO<sub>2</sub>) are produced during its production via the oxidation of olivine and pyroxene (mafic and ultra-mafic rocks) by water [1]
          <ul class="sub-list">
            <li>Chemolithotrophic microbial communities rely on H<sub>2</sub> and CH<sub>4</sub> nutrient sources [2]</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>Serpentine has characteristics absorption bands at 1.38, 2.12, 2.32, and 2.52 &micro;m at near infrared wavelengths [3]
          <ul class="sub-list">
            <li>2.12 &micro;m is the most characteristic band for a unique identification (Fig. 1)</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>Identifying serpentine on Mars using I/F data from the Compact Reconnaissance Imaging Spectrometer for Mars (CRISM) is challenging due to the presence of an artifact in the images [4]
          <ul class="sub-list">
            <li>Requires a validation of &nbsp;2.12 &micro;m band in radiance data &ndash; called radiance ratio technique [4] (Fig. 2)</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="fig-row">
        <div class="fig-block">
          <div class="placeholder" style="width:100%;height:150px;">[Chart: Fig. 1 &ndash; laboratory reflectance spectra plot, wavelength 1.2&ndash;2.6 &micro;m vs reflectance, curves for serpentine, dolomite, chlorite]</div>
          <div class="fig-caption"><b>Fig. 1</b>: Laboratory spectra of serpentine, dolomite, and Mg-rich chlorite (RELAB database [5]).</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fig-block">
          <div class="placeholder" style="width:100%;height:150px;">[Photo: crater image with arrow marking claimed serpentine pixel location (A)]</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fig-block">
          <div class="placeholder" style="width:100%;height:150px;">[Chart: I/F and ratio spectra (offset) plot labeled B, showing artifact peak and 2.12 marker]</div>
          <div class="fig-caption"><b>Fig. 2</b>: Example of a false positive serpentine detection on Mars using CRISM data. (a) Location of serpentine claimed pixels. (b) Spectra showing the presence of a 2.12 &micro;m feature in the I/F data but absent in the radiance spectrum [6].</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div style="flex:1;">
      <div class="section-title" style="margin-top:0;">OBJECTIVE</div>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li>Create an updated global map of serpentine for Mars using CRISM images
          <ul class="sub-list check-list">
            <li>using factor analysis target transform method [6]</li>
            <li>validated with both I/F and <i>radiance</i> data</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>Geologic context and mineral assemblage associated with serpentine</li>
        <li>Timing and amount (approx.) of H<sub>2</sub> produced via serpentinization on Mars</li>
        <li>Extend of serpentinization on the surface and subsurface</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="section-title">METHODOLOGY</div>
      <ul class="method-list">
        <li>A total of 15,760 CRISM Targeted Reduced Data Record (TDRR) images
          <ul class="sub-list">
            <li>Uses all TRDR records viz. 7885 Full Resolution Targeted (FRT), 2785 Half Resolution Long (HRL), 3738 Full Resolution Short (FRS), 1352 and Half Resolution Short (HRS)</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>Covers 90&deg; N to 90&deg; S latitudes (~1.2% of Martian surface)</li>
        <li>Atmospherically and photometrically corrected using the standard CRISM processing pipeline including volcano-scan calibration [7]</li>
        <li>Apply dynamic aperture factor analysis/target transformation (DAFA/TT) [6] to the calibrated CRISM images</li>
        <li>I/F and radiance ratio spectra from the DAFA/TT detected pixels</li>
        <li>Detections are assigned to confidence values&mdash; 1 (low/weak) to 5 (high/best)</li>
        <li>Geologic context using CTX and THEMIS-IR daytime mosaics [8-9]</li>
        <li>Age of serpentine host unit [10] and associated minerals from CRISM</li>
        <li>Influence of dust in surface detection using dust cover index map [11]</li>
        <li>Amount of H<sub>2</sub> produced using the rates from analogue rocks [12]</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-title">RESULTS</div>

  <div class="fig-row" style="align-items:flex-start;">
    <div style="flex:0 0 18%;">
      <div class="placeholder" style="width:100%;height:110px;">[Chart: bar chart, No. of CRISM images by longitude bins with legend]</div>
      <div class="placeholder" style="width:100%;height:150px;margin-top:5px;">[Map: global serpentine distribution overlain on MOLA topography, colored by confidence, labeled A]</div>
      <div class="placeholder" style="width:100%;height:60px;margin-top:5px;">[Chart: dust cover rainbow scale bar, min to max dust]</div>
    </div>
    <div style="flex:0 0 27%;">
      <div class="placeholder" style="width:100%;height:150px;margin-top:117px;">[Map: regional context map with boxes B and C highlighted, scale 0-50 Km]</div>
    </div>
    <div style="flex:0 0 27%;">
      <div class="placeholder" style="width:100%;height:150px;margin-top:117px;">[Photo: crater CTX image B with red box inset, scale 1 Km]</div>
      <div class="placeholder" style="width:100%;height:150px;margin-top:5px;">[Photo: crater CTX image C with red box inset, scale 10 Km]</div>
    </div>
    <div style="flex:0 0 27%;">
      <div class="placeholder" style="width:100%;height:300px;margin-top:117px;">[Chart D: ratio of I/F reflectance vs wavelength, multiple colored spectra curves (crater, halo, radiance, serpentine, Mg-carbonate, Mg-chlorite), labeled hr0000860c_07_if181i]</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="fig-caption">
    <b>Fig. 3</b>: Global distribution of serpentine on Mars overlain on the TES dust cover map on MOLA global mosaic [12]. Serpentine detections are color-coded according to confidence level. Upper left inset shows number of images investigated in 30&deg; longitudinal bin.
    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
    <b>Fig. 4</b>: a) Black outline represents HRL0000860C on CTX image. Upper left inset shows the regional context. b) and c) show the local geologic context (red polygons) on CTX. d) The average I/F (blue) and radiance (orange) ratio spectra of both pixel clusters.
  </div>

  <div class="key-findings-box">
    <div class="key-findings-title">## Key findings ##</div>
    <div style="display:flex; gap:25px;">
      <div style="flex:1.3;">
        <ul class="doc-list">
          <li>Detected in 43 CRISM images, with detections in 32 of the images representing "new" or previously unrecognized detections</li>
          <li>Occurrence between 40&deg;N to 40&deg;S latitudes, and no detections between 90&deg;E to 180&deg;E longitudes</li>
          <li>Formation in a wide variety of landforms (craters, valleys, and plains)</li>
          <li>31 out of 43 detected images on Noachian-aged surface units</li>
          <li>Younger detections are associated with impact craters&mdash; possibility that serpentine formed earlier at depth, and was subsequently excavated</li>
          <li>Serpentine detections correspond with lower average dust cover</li>
          <li>Often occurs near olivine, low-Ca pyroxene, clays, and carbonate minerals</li>
          <li>Mg-rich serpentine detections cover a total surface area of ~1.46 km&sup2;</li>
          <li>Estimated max. production (~10 km) of 5.11 &times; 10<sup>11</sup> and 4.85 &times; 10<sup>13</sup> mol H<sub>2</sub></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div style="flex:1; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:6px;">
        <div class="placeholder" style="width:100%;height:100px;">[Diagram: flow/network diagram showing Craters, Valleys, Plains counts (19, 16, 8) and sub-categories]</div>
        <div class="placeholder" style="width:100%;height:60px;">[Chart A: bar+line, Total images investigated vs detected by geologic age]</div>
      </div>
      <div style="flex:1; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:6px; margin-top:106px;">
        <div class="placeholder" style="width:100%;height:130px;">[Chart B: bar+line, # of CRISM images vs TES Dust Index by geologic age]</div>
      </div>
      <div style="flex:1; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:6px; margin-top:106px;">
        <div class="placeholder" style="width:100%;height:130px;">[Chart C: bar+line, # of detections and TES Dust Index Avg by longitude]</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="fig-caption">
      <b>Fig. 5</b>: Characteristics of serpentine deposits on Mars. (a) Geologic setting of serpentine deposits. (b) Number of images investigated, binned by the age of units they occur, plotted with the average TES dust index values (right axis). (c) Longitudinal distribution of average TES dust index values and the number of CRISM images containing serpentine. Higher TES dust index values correspond to less dusty regions.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-title">DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSION</div>
  <div class="discussion-grid">
    <div>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li>Only 11 CRISM images previously thought to contain serpentine [13] could be validated for the presence of the mineral using the radiance ratio technique</li>
        <li>Most serpentine deposits identified here occur in Noachian-aged terrains implying that serpentinization occurred predominantly early in Mars' history</li>
        <li>Although it occurs in a wide variety of landscapes, the detection of serpentine in craters raises the possibility that impact-induced hydrothermal serpentinization was an important process for serpentine formation</li>
        <li>Although serpentinization is favored thermodynamically in mafic/ultramafic rocks on the surface and in the subsurface, Mg-serpentine deposits was found to be generally rare and of relatively limited areal extent</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p style="margin:4px 0;">Reasons for the apparent paucity of serpentine on Mars could include:</p>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li>a). Limitations of orbiter sensor capability and the impact of dust and/or sub-pixel mixing</li>
        <li>b). Inability to uniquely detect Fe-rich serpentine on Mars [16]</li>
        <li>c). CRISM data covers only ~1.2% of the Martian surface</li>
        <li>d). Serpentine may once have formed extensively on early Mars, but has subsequently weathered and altered to clay minerals and carbonates</li>
        <li>e). Serpentinization occurred primarily in the subsurface and is observable only when excavated by impact cratering or other tectonic processes</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-title">REFERENCES</div>
  <p style="font-size:11px; margin: 4px 0 10px 0;">*References are provided upon request.</p>

</div>

